## Identified Debate Gaps (unaddressed)
The following gaps were identified mid-debate but have NOT yet been substantively addressed by any debater. Prioritize steering the conversation toward these:
  1. [blind_spot] The program's verification backbone — mandatory hardware-level telemetry and population-scale output logging across all deployed AI, held in government-accessible escrow — is itself the largest computational surveillance infrastructure ever proposed, and once built it will be repurposed beyond its safety mandate (law-enforcement subpoenas of inference logs, national-security carve-outs, chilling of private and dissident uses of AI). The debate treats verification capacity as costless safety-neutral plumbing whose only failure mode is being insufficiently rigorous; no one has priced the risk that the monitoring apparatus succeeds and becomes an instrument of state or incumbent power over all computation. (Why missing: All three debaters are competing to make the telemetry MORE pervasive, not questioning its existence: the Accelerationist proposed it as the answer to the paperwork critique, the Safetyist demands it be tightened with provenance mandates and forensic access, and the Skeptic wants claimants and auditors granted broader access to the logs (privacy 'written in' as an afterthought clause). Each perspective's core commitment — deployment speed, deterrence, or victim recourse — is served by expanding the apparatus, so none has an incentive to argue that verifiable-compliance infrastructure is dual-use and that its capture by future administrations or intelligence agencies may be a larger correlated harm than the AI failures it monitors. The Boeing/MCAS and capture arguments were all aimed at the gate being too weak, never at the gate being too powerful.)
  2. [unstated_assumption] The excise-tax mechanism assumes a taxable unit that does not exist for AI: the VICP works because a vaccine dose is a discrete, countable, single-manufacturer unit taxed at $0.75, whereas 'AI services' flow through multi-vendor pipelines (base model, fine-tuner, API reseller, deploying enterprise, open-weight forks) with no natural unit of sale — and a risk-tiered tax that prices healthcare and diagnostic AI highest will pass through to exactly the high-value deployments the program claims to accelerate, while untaxed low-tier and offshore or open-weight deployments free-ride on the fund. Before debating gate design, Congress must answer: taxed per what, collected from whom in the supply chain, and why won't tier-differentiated pricing push capital toward the lowest-tier (least beneficial) uses? (Why missing: The Accelerationist cannot raise it because the excise tax is the load-bearing funding mechanism for the auditors, the board, and the compensation fund — conceding the unit problem unravels the whole fiscal architecture, and conceding the incidence problem (taxing medical AI hardest) contradicts the abundance thesis that high-stakes deployment must be cheapest and fastest. The Safetyist actively wants high-risk tiers to be expensive, so tax-induced deterrence of healthcare AI reads to them as a feature, not a distortion worth flagging. The Skeptic attacked who defines the tiers and what the metrics miss, but proposed rerouting the same tax through an independent trust — implicitly accepting that it is collectible. All three have argued about how to spend the money without establishing that it can be levied on an industry whose product boundary, unlike a vaccine dose, is not enumerable.)


